From: Moussa Ouallaf <tramoryp@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] ENH add `offset` to LogisticRegression and the GLM regressors (#24614)

Adds a fixed per-sample offset to the raw prediction,

    raw_prediction = X @ coef + intercept + offset,

passed as an array of shape (n_samples,) to `fit` and to every prediction
method, like `sample_weight` (option 3 in scikit-learn issue #24614, the API
statsmodels, glmnet, R and glum use). The offset is data: it is never stored,
and `offset=None` is the current behaviour bit for bit.

Use cases: an exposure term in count models (`offset=np.log(exposure)` with
PoissonRegressor); coefficients known in advance (a fixed offset
`v * X[:, j]` is coefficient j fixed at v); starting from an existing model's
log-odds and learning only the correction other features add; the case-control
prior correction of the intercept (King and Zeng 2001), which is a constant
offset.

Changes
- `LinearModelLoss(offset=None)`: the offset enters every loss, gradient,
  Hessian and Hessian-product through `weight_intercept_raw`, which gains
  `add_offset=True`.
- `NewtonSolver.line_search`: the Newton direction is linear in the
  coefficients and is now computed with `add_offset=False`. Without this,
  `raw(coef + t * d)` counted the offset twice per step and newton-cholesky
  stalled or fell back to lbfgs whenever the offset was nonzero.
- `LogisticRegression.fit / decision_function / predict / predict_proba /
  predict_log_proba(offset=None)`: binary problems with the solvers lbfgs,
  newton-cg and newton-cholesky; other solvers and multiclass raise before
  liblinear's early return. Metadata routing works through the usual
  `set_fit_request(offset=True)` / `set_predict_proba_request(offset=True)`.
- `LogisticRegressionCV.fit(offset=None)`: each fold fits on its training part
  of the offset and scores with its test part; the refit uses all of it.
- `_GeneralizedLinearRegressor.fit / predict / score(offset=None)` for
  PoissonRegressor, GammaRegressor and TweedieRegressor (on the link scale).

Tests: zero offset equals no offset; the fit minimises the stated objective
(direct scipy minimisation) for every supported solver; a constant offset only
moves the intercept; C -> 0 keeps the offset's own prediction; newton-cholesky
converges with a large offset; prediction methods add the offset; validation;
sparse and float32; clone / pickle; pipeline routing; LogisticRegressionCV
equals a manual fold loop; Poisson with offset log(exposure) equals the rate
model with sample_weight = exposure. Equality with statsmodels GLM(offset=) and
glum to 1e-6 is checked outside this patch (it needs those packages).

Base: scikit-learn 1.9.1. The glm.py hunks carry no context lines: apply with
    git apply --unidiff-zero scikit-learn-offset.patch
or `patch -p1 < scikit-learn-offset.patch`.

